      },
      "rev_n_3_d62b27_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_d62b27_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_d62b27",
        "thought_text": "Develop a backtracking algorithm to place the smaller polyominoes within the larger grid, ensuring they fit without overlapping and cover all 'X's. However, before attempting to place a polyomino, check if there are enough remaining cells to accommodate it, avoiding unnecessary recursive calls and potential errors.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_d62b27_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_bd46ee1eed",
        "created_at": 1794504586.881287
      },
      "rev_n_4_dd89da_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_dd89da_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_dd89da",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through each cell in the grid and try to identify potential starting points for smaller polyominoes. For each starting point, attempt to grow the polyomino in all four directions until it reaches the required size. If successful, mark the cells as part of the identified polyomino and continue with the next cell. This approach ensures that we explore all possible configurations.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6693871521376781897",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794504604.427435
      },
      "rev_n_5_7dc20a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_7dc20a_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_7dc20a",
        "thought_text": "Use a depth-first search (DFS) algorithm to explore all possible ways to divide the polyomino into smaller polyominoes. Start from each cell and recursively try to place smaller polyominoes of increasing sizes. If at any point a valid division is found, return the result. If no valid division is found after exploring all possibilities, return 'No solution'. This method guarantees finding a solution if one exists but may be computationally expensive for larger grids.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6693871521376781897",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794504604.4274504
      },
      "rev_n_6_0788d8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_0788d8_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_0788d8",
        "thought_text": "Apply a dynamic programming approach where the state represents whether a subgrid can be divided into a specific number of smaller polyominoes. Use a memoization table to store results of subproblems to avoid redundant calculations. Initialize the table with the entire grid and iteratively reduce the problem size by trying to divide the grid into fewer and fewer smaller polyominoes. If a valid division is found for a particular state, propagate this information back up the table. This approach balances between time complexity and memory usage, making it suitable for moderate-sized grids.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6693871521376781897",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794504604.4274592
      },
